WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.767 --> 00:00:02.801
-That feel good to get
a standing ovation?

00:00:02.801 --> 00:00:04.634
-It sure does.
Thank you very much.

00:00:04.634 --> 00:00:06.501
-Welcome back!
-Thank you.

00:00:06.501 --> 00:00:07.534
[ Applause ]

00:00:07.534 --> 00:00:09.367
I love having you on.
-Thank you.

00:00:09.367 --> 00:00:10.701
-You're a friend to
"The Tonight Show."

00:00:10.701 --> 00:00:11.667
You've been on
"The Tonight Show"

00:00:11.667 --> 00:00:13.467
with Jack Paar.
-Yes.

00:00:13.467 --> 00:00:16.901
-You've been on with Johnny
Carson, Jay Leno, and now me.

00:00:16.901 --> 00:00:17.968
-And you.
-Yeah.

00:00:17.968 --> 00:00:19.467
And I'm so happy that
you're here.

00:00:19.467 --> 00:00:22.100
-Thank you. Thank you.
-Oh, please. I'm honored.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.000
You were given
a Lifetime Achievement Award

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.033
by the American Film Institute.

00:00:26.033 --> 00:00:28.634
-I was, last week.
-Last week?

00:00:28.634 --> 00:00:30.300
[ Applause ]

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.400
It's quite an honor.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:36.300
-It's a huge honor, and I was
absolutely thrilled.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.801
And they spoiled me, and so many
people came that night.

00:00:40.801 --> 00:00:45.067
-Your speech was so touching
and so cool, I thought.

00:00:45.067 --> 00:00:46.634
-Oh, thank you.

00:00:46.634 --> 00:00:50.000
-It wasn't about you,
which I thought was interesting.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.100
You didn't make it about you.
You wanted to thank the crew.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:54.934
-Well, you know,

00:00:54.934 --> 00:00:58.267
I actually said that
nobody acknowledges,

00:00:58.267 --> 00:01:01.667
except in the credits
that run by far too fast,

00:01:01.667 --> 00:01:02.567
as well well know.

00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:04.634
-Yes.
-Especially on TV.

00:01:04.634 --> 00:01:07.667
I wish something could be
done about that.

00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:10.968
I just wanted to acknowledge
everybody in the studio,

00:01:10.968 --> 00:01:14.534
and there are hundreds of people
behind the camera,

00:01:14.534 --> 00:01:17.734
and nobody ever recognizes them,
so I wanted to do that.

00:01:17.734 --> 00:01:19.133
-Yeah.

00:01:19.133 --> 00:01:21.434
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:21.434 --> 00:01:22.601
-It's true.

00:01:22.601 --> 00:01:25.701
-There's so many people
that make the film

00:01:25.701 --> 00:01:26.834
and make everyone look good.

00:01:26.834 --> 00:01:29.300
You said that there was
a set designer,

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:30.667
or a painter, maybe?

00:01:30.667 --> 00:01:32.701
-It's a true story.

00:01:32.701 --> 00:01:36.634
I was driving home one day
off the lot

00:01:36.634 --> 00:01:37.968
at 20th Century Fox,

00:01:37.968 --> 00:01:41.067
and I was finishing
a long day of shooting

00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:42.834
on "The Sound of Music."

00:01:42.834 --> 00:01:46.734
And I passed a gentleman, as I
got to the back gate of the lot,

00:01:46.734 --> 00:01:48.501
which was the quick way home,
for me.

00:01:48.501 --> 00:01:51.467
I passed a gentleman
who was painting

00:01:51.467 --> 00:01:53.200
in a sort of open shed.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:55.267
And he smiled at me as I waited

00:01:55.267 --> 00:01:56.868
for the gate
to open at the back,

00:01:56.868 --> 00:01:59.501
and I rolled down my window
and I said,

00:01:59.501 --> 00:02:01.634
"You're working a bit late
tonight, aren't you?"

00:02:01.634 --> 00:02:04.400
And he came over
and talked to me,

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:06.901
and he told me how proud he was

00:02:06.901 --> 00:02:09.200
and how long he'd been
working at the studio.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.934
And he said, almost boasting,

00:02:11.934 --> 00:02:16.033
"You know, I've painted sets
for Marilyn Monroe

00:02:16.033 --> 00:02:18.367
and Jack Lemmon
and Shirley MacLaine."

00:02:18.367 --> 00:02:20.434
And I suddenly thought, my God.

00:02:20.434 --> 00:02:24.567
That's what made me want to
acknowledge everybody,

00:02:24.567 --> 00:02:28.033
because he was so thrilled
to be a part of film,

00:02:28.033 --> 00:02:30.634
and I don't think anybody
knew he was there.

00:02:30.634 --> 00:02:31.767
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:31.767 --> 00:02:33.100
-Exactly, and this business,

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:35.634
it's just great to
acknowledge everybody.

00:02:35.634 --> 00:02:37.534
-Well, it takes a lot of people.

00:02:37.534 --> 00:02:41.033
It's all about -- it's here,
everywhere, about collaboration.

00:02:41.033 --> 00:02:44.767
-So you did this, and
immediately after the ceremony,

00:02:44.767 --> 00:02:47.133
you flew to your granddaughter's
high school graduation.

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:48.901
-Yes, the next day.

00:02:48.901 --> 00:02:52.701
-And you nailed it, and she's
going to study theater and film.

00:02:52.701 --> 00:02:55.300
-Yep, she's going to
Sarah Lawrence,

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:58.734
and she's going to study
theater and film

00:02:58.734 --> 00:03:00.801
and literature,
and all the rest.

00:03:00.801 --> 00:03:03.767
-Do you give advice?

00:03:03.767 --> 00:03:07.267
-A little bit, but she's got
some kind of savvy

00:03:07.267 --> 00:03:08.467
that I don't know where it --

00:03:08.467 --> 00:03:10.467
I mean, I do know
where it comes from, I guess.

00:03:10.467 --> 00:03:12.200
[ Laughter ]

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:13.634
But she seems to know
a lot already.

00:03:13.634 --> 00:03:16.000
-Yeah.
-She's very, very smart,

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.334
and very talented.

00:03:17.334 --> 00:03:20.601
-Do your grandchildren realize
that their grandmother

00:03:20.601 --> 00:03:21.968
is Mary Poppins?

00:03:21.968 --> 00:03:24.267
-Well, now they do, but --

00:03:24.267 --> 00:03:26.133
I think.
-Yeah.

00:03:26.133 --> 00:03:30.801
When my grandson, my lovely
young girl's brother,

00:03:30.801 --> 00:03:33.300
was very young,
he was about 4, I guess,

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:36.567
and they were showing "Mary
Poppins" at a birthday party

00:03:36.567 --> 00:03:37.767
that he went to.

00:03:37.767 --> 00:03:41.367
When his mother came
to pick him up,

00:03:41.367 --> 00:03:43.667
he was standing this close
to the television,

00:03:43.667 --> 00:03:45.534
looking terribly puzzled.

00:03:45.534 --> 00:03:48.434
[ Laughter ]
And my daughter bent down

00:03:48.434 --> 00:03:50.400
and said, "Do you know
who that lady is?"

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:52.834
And he went, "Uh-uh."

00:03:52.834 --> 00:03:55.901
And she said, "Is it maybe
somebody that we know

00:03:55.901 --> 00:03:57.534
very well?"

00:03:57.534 --> 00:03:58.667
"Uh-uh."

00:03:58.667 --> 00:04:01.934
And she said, "Is it maybe
Granny Jules?"

00:04:01.934 --> 00:04:03.634
And he went, "Oh, yes!"

00:04:03.634 --> 00:04:05.667
[ Laughter ]

00:04:05.667 --> 00:04:07.601
He was thrilled.

00:04:07.601 --> 00:04:08.701
-Isn't that great?

00:04:08.701 --> 00:04:11.467
-But the best part was,
the next day,

00:04:11.467 --> 00:04:14.467
when I went over to the house
to see him, he said,

00:04:14.467 --> 00:04:16.501
"Hello, Granny Jules."

00:04:16.501 --> 00:04:19.300
And it was with such
a knowing look,

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:20.801
and "I know what you do."

00:04:20.801 --> 00:04:22.601
[ Laughter ]

00:04:22.601 --> 00:04:25.000
It was adorable. You know with
kids, it's so sweet.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:26.400
Yeah.
-When people come up to you

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:30.968
on the street, do they recognize
you for different films?

00:04:30.968 --> 00:04:34.868
-They certainly do these days,
but it's interesting.

00:04:34.868 --> 00:04:37.000
It depends what age they are,
Jimmy.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:38.234
If it's a young kid, it's,

00:04:38.234 --> 00:04:40.267
"Oh, I loved you
in 'Princess Diaries.'"

00:04:40.267 --> 00:04:41.567
-Yes!

00:04:41.567 --> 00:04:42.968
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:42.968 --> 00:04:44.934
-But...

00:04:44.934 --> 00:04:48.067
But if it's an older lady

00:04:48.067 --> 00:04:49.667
or a granny or somebody
like that,

00:04:49.667 --> 00:04:52.734
it's "I adored you in
'Sound of Music,'" or so on.

00:04:52.734 --> 00:04:54.501
And it just depends.

00:04:54.501 --> 00:04:55.901
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:04:55.901 --> 00:04:59.100
-You have such a great career,
but it is interesting that

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:00.467
probably the number one question

00:05:00.467 --> 00:05:02.400
at all the interviews
I've watched you do recently is,

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:05.434
everyone asks you, "Is there
gonna be 'Princess Diaries 3'?"

00:05:05.434 --> 00:05:10.100
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:11.901
-Honestly, I doubt it,

00:05:11.901 --> 00:05:15.400
because I'm that much older,
and Annie's that much older.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:17.033
Annie Hathaway,
who was so lovely.

00:05:17.033 --> 00:05:19.667
-Yeah.
-There was talk about it

00:05:19.667 --> 00:05:20.767
many, many years ago,

00:05:20.767 --> 00:05:23.033
but I think it's gone
too far now.

00:05:23.033 --> 00:05:24.634
I'd be thrilled
if it would happen,

00:05:24.634 --> 00:05:26.634
but I can't think what
the story would be now.

00:05:26.634 --> 00:05:27.834
-Yeah.

00:05:27.834 --> 00:05:30.501
Let's talk about
another blockbuster,

00:05:30.501 --> 00:05:31.367
a children's hit.

00:05:31.367 --> 00:05:32.834
"Minions: The Rise of Gru."

00:05:32.834 --> 00:05:35.434
-Yes!
[ Applause ]

00:05:35.434 --> 00:05:37.834
-It's out July 1st.

00:05:37.834 --> 00:05:39.567
-Aren't those Minions adorable?

00:05:39.567 --> 00:05:40.767
Don't you love them?

00:05:40.767 --> 00:05:42.767
-How do they do it?
It's so genius.

00:05:42.767 --> 00:05:45.067
-There's one that just
says, "Hello,"

00:05:45.067 --> 00:05:48.467
something like that,
and he's adorable.

00:05:48.467 --> 00:05:49.968
-He's so cute, yeah.
-Oh, yeah.

00:05:49.968 --> 00:05:54.033
-I saw it with my girls,
they're 8 and 7,

00:05:54.033 --> 00:05:55.067
and they loved it.

00:05:55.067 --> 00:05:56.234
Do you like playing a villain?

00:05:56.234 --> 00:05:57.834
-[ Laughing ] Yes.

00:05:57.834 --> 00:06:01.100
Gru's mum is one of
the worst characters

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:02.801
you could possibly imagine.

00:06:02.801 --> 00:06:05.834
She's the worst role model
for a mother.

00:06:05.834 --> 00:06:07.200
She's terrible to Gru,

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:10.868
and it was a delight to
get my teeth into it,

00:06:10.868 --> 00:06:12.033
I have to admit.

00:06:12.033 --> 00:06:13.934
-[ Laughing ] You nailed it.
You nailed it.

00:06:13.934 --> 00:06:16.467
[ Applause ]

00:06:16.467 --> 00:06:17.868
It's so perfect.

00:06:17.868 --> 00:06:19.200
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:21.400
Here's Julie Andrews in
"Minions: The Rise of Gru."

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:22.534
-Oh, you angel.
-Take a look.

00:06:22.534 --> 00:06:24.100
-Thank you.

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:26.167
-[ Cackling ]
-Mom?

00:06:26.167 --> 00:06:28.667
-Blahhh.

00:06:28.667 --> 00:06:30.734
♪♪

00:06:30.734 --> 00:06:33.567
Blahhh.

00:06:33.567 --> 00:06:35.701
Blahhhh!

00:06:35.701 --> 00:06:37.267
-What?

00:06:37.267 --> 00:06:39.567
Mom, who is this sweaty guy?

00:06:39.567 --> 00:06:41.234
He's stinking up the house.

00:06:41.234 --> 00:06:43.000
I thought you were
cooking cabbage.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:45.267
-He's my new guru.

00:06:45.267 --> 00:06:47.234
-Blehhh.
-[ Chuckles ]

00:06:47.234 --> 00:06:49.234
-Blahhh!
-Bllll!

00:06:49.234 --> 00:06:51.400
-Tell your weird buddies they
better start

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:53.300
pulling their weight
around here,

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:54.400
or I'm kicking them out!

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:57.200
They are killing
my mellow vibes!

00:06:57.200 --> 00:06:58.400
-Uh...

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:00.300
-Bleh-bleh-bleh-bleh.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:01.968
-Hey! I heard that.

00:07:01.968 --> 00:07:04.667
[ Laughing ]
-That's right!

00:07:04.667 --> 00:07:06.234
Julie Andrews,
ladies and gentlemen!

00:07:06.234 --> 00:07:09.501
"Minions: The Rise of Gru"
is in theaters July 1st.

00:07:09.501 --> 00:07:10.801
-Thank you so much.

00:07:10.801 --> 00:07:12.467
-We'll be right back
with more "Tonight Show."

